\section{Required columns}{


\code{episodic_case_columns} lists all fifteen, in order. The set is an
explicit allow-list: a column outside it, or one missing from it, is
rejected by \code{\link[=episodic_validate_cases]{episodic_validate_cases()}} rather than silently ignored.
"Required" means the column must be present; several may be \code{NA}
throughout if your laboratory does not record them.

Three columns accept only a fixed set of values, given as
\code{episodic_care_lines}, \code{episodic_institution_types} and
\code{episodic_sex_codes} so you can map onto them in your extract step
rather than copying the strings out of this page.

\describe{
\item{\code{source_key}}{Character, required, no \code{NA}, unique within the
data set. A stable identifier for this result in your own source
system, so re-running the same extract later cannot create
duplicate cases. Any string will do, as long as the same result
always carries the same one.}
\item{\code{patient_key}}{Character, required, no \code{NA}. A pseudonymised
patient identifier, the same for the same patient across results.
Deduplication and episode grouping key on it: without it every
isolate becomes its own case. Never shown as-is in the dashboard,
but do pseudonymise it before it reaches EpiSODIC - a BSN or
hospital number must not be passed through.}
\item{\code{sample_date}}{Date, or character in \code{YYYY-MM-DD} (ISO 8601)
form. Required, no \code{NA}. The date the sample was taken, and the
anchor every detector, trend and report is built against. If your
system falls back to a receipt date when the sample date is
unfilled, apply that fallback in your own extract step, not here.}
\item{\code{receipt_date}}{Date, or character in \code{YYYY-MM-DD} form; \code{NA}
allowed. The date the laboratory received the sample. Stored for
provenance only - deliberately never used to measure reporting
delay, since a lab's receipt-date field can itself be standing in
for a missing sample date.}
\item{\code{pathogen}}{Character, required, no \code{NA}. The pathogen name
exactly as your laboratory reports it (e.g. \code{"Escherichia coli"},
\code{"Influenza A"}, \code{"Norovirus"}). Free text, deliberately
unconstrained: used verbatim and never matched against a taxonomy,
since detection has to work for anything a lab can report, viral or
not. Spelling must be stable across runs - \code{"Influenza A"} and
\code{"influenza a"} are two different pathogens as far as detection is
concerned. Names matching \code{inst/config/pathogen_config.csv} pick up
that pathogen's episode length, incubation window and serial
interval; anything else falls back to the schema defaults (30-day
episode, 14 case-free days, no Rt, no MEM). The same isolate may
appear under more than one \code{pathogen} value where that is
epidemiologically useful - an ETEC isolate reported as both
\code{"Escherichia coli"} and \code{"ETEC"}, so each is monitored separately.}
\item{\code{care_line}}{Character; \code{NA} allowed. One of \code{"first"}
(primary care), \code{"second"} (secondary care), \code{"other"}, or
\code{"unknown"} - the values in \code{episodic_care_lines}. Anything else is
rejected. \code{NA} is read as \code{"unknown"} and stored that way, so you
need not map missing values yourself: an empty \code{care_line}, an
R \code{NA} and a database \code{NULL} all mean the same thing here, and the
dashboard shows all three as "unknown".}
\item{\code{institution_key}}{Character, required, no \code{NA}. A stable
identifier for the reporting institution. Hashed (SHA-1) on the way
in, so a later rename does not fracture the institution's history,
and so the raw key never reaches the database.}
\item{\code{institution_display_name}}{Character, required, no \code{NA}. The
human-readable institution name shown in the dashboard.}
\item{\code{institution_type}}{Character, required. Exactly one of
\code{"hospital"}, \code{"ltc_institution"} (long-term care),
\code{"gp_municipality"}, \code{"ooh_service"} (out-of-hours service), or
\code{"other"} - the values in \code{episodic_institution_types}. Anything
else is rejected. This decides how the institution is handled:
\code{"hospital"} institutions
are monitored as first-class entities and are the only ones
eligible for patient-day normalisation, while a \code{"gp_municipality"}
is collapsed to its municipality.}
\item{\code{municipality}}{Character; \code{NA} allowed. The institution's
municipality. Required in practice for \code{"gp_municipality"} rows,
since that is what they are collapsed to.}
\item{\code{ward}}{Character; \code{NA} allowed. The ward within the
institution. Only meaningful for \code{"hospital"} and
\code{"ltc_institution"} rows; leave \code{NA} otherwise. This is the unit L1
(ward-level) detection watches, so an inconsistent spelling splits
one ward into two streams.}
\item{\code{specialism}}{Character; \code{NA} allowed. The requesting clinical
